## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of practical, beginner-friendly schema evolution across HTTP input, DB writes, and Kafka events without breaking existing consumers.

## Key Concepts

- Backward-compatible schema evolution
- Optional/additive fields and defaults
- Schema registry with compatibility levels
- Canary rollout and feature flags
- Contract and end-to-end testing

## Code Example

```javascript
const Ajv = require("ajv");
const ajv = new Ajv();
const schema = {
  type: "object",
  properties: {
    id: { type: "string" },
    event: { type: "string" },
    customer_segment: { type: "string" }
  },
  required: ["id", "event"],
  additionalProperties: true
};
function validate(payload){
  const valid = ajv.validate(schema, payload);
  if(!valid) throw new Error(ajv.errors.map(e=>e.message).join(", "));
  return payload;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you handle a future breaking change if a client needs the new field immediately?
- How would you monitor and alert on schema compatibility errors in prod?

## Why This Is Asked

Test practical tracing and end-to-end visibility across CNPA during retries and regional outages, ensuring the correlation persists across services and layers.

## Key Concepts

- Correlation IDs / distributed tracing across HTTP, DB, Kafka, Redis
- Middleware propagation, headers, and context propagation
- Retry semantics, idempotent writes, and rollback strategies
- Observability: logs, metrics, traces, dashboards

## Code Example

```javascript
// Express middleware to ensure correlation ID propagation
app.use((req, res, next) => {
  const id = req.headers['x-correlation-id'] || uuidv4();
  req.correlationId = id;
  res.setHeader('X-Correlation-Id', id);
  next();
});
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you verify the correlation ID is preserved through PostgreSQL writes and Kafka messages?
- How would you test this under retries and partial outages, including automated end-to-end tests?
